WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:04.879
Okay, so let's go ahead and get going with the exercise for this course.

2
00:00:06.219 --> 00:00:10.060
In this case, I've opened up the map in one of the projects that was given to you

3
00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:14.380
as part of this course. So if you go and open up your Lumen project,

4
00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:21.260
and you go to Lumen example, and open up the map, you should be able to see the same thing

5
00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:26.190
I'm seeing here. Now, before I do anything, I wanted to go over all the light types that we

6
00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:32.399
have in Unreal Engine to show you how they work individually, as well as some of the details

7
00:00:32.479 --> 00:00:37.119
that are more general about lighting in general. So in order to do this, what I'm going to do is

8
00:00:37.119 --> 00:00:41.280
just delete all the lights that are here, and I'll do one by one, and sort of take a look at them.

9
00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:46.270
So I'm going to delete the two skylights and direction lights here, and you can see

10
00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:50.189
we're going to complete darkness, except for these emissive materials, which are contributing

11
00:00:50.189 --> 00:00:57.259
to the Lumen scene, as we mentioned earlier in the slides. So let's start with the first

12
00:00:57.259 --> 00:01:01.700
light here. I've gone to the Place Actors tab and gone to the lights, and I'm going to

13
00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:04.739
start with the first light, which is the direction light. So I'm just going to drag that in,

14
00:01:04.739 --> 00:01:09.700
and you can see it already does the work of a direction light. So this is one of the

15
00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:15.890
main lights. It's essentially going to work like your sunlight or your moonlight. Now, I can rotate

16
00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:22.689
or move, but in this case, for the direction light, moving doesn't make a difference because

17
00:01:22.689 --> 00:01:28.049
it's a bunch of parallel rays emulating the sun. But in the case for the direction light,

18
00:01:28.049 --> 00:01:31.569
rotating is going to be your friend in terms of getting the shadows that you want.

19
00:01:32.769 --> 00:01:40.530
Let's go ahead and do a pretty low light and get some shadows on the wall here. And the reason I'm

20
00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:46.290
trying to get some shadows on the wall is just because it'll help us play around with some of

21
00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:54.219
the features. So here we go. We have our lights up. And importantly, if we're going to work with

22
00:01:54.219 --> 00:01:58.379
Lumen, it's better to have our lights as movable. So on every light type that you use,

23
00:01:58.379 --> 00:02:03.900
or every static mesh as well, you will see here the mobility of the actor. Now, in our case,

24
00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:09.939
as I say, we want this to be movable, so we'll set that to movable. And there you have it. Now,

25
00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:14.979
with all lights, we have things like the intensity values or the color of the light

26
00:02:14.979 --> 00:02:18.259
that we can change. For example, if we wanted to make this a little bit more orange,

27
00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:24.750
we could infuse a bit of orange light in there. We could also change the intensity,

28
00:02:28.819 --> 00:02:34.240
leave that at 10, as well as some other properties like the indirect light and intensity,

29
00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:41.580
so how much it contributes to indirect lighting, for example. We'll leave these at one. And one

30
00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:44.539
of the nice tricks about the direction light that I wanted to show you is that if you're trying to

31
00:02:44.539 --> 00:02:50.060
soften your shadows up, the source angle here is the value you're trying to find. So if I drag

32
00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:56.020
this up, you'll notice that my shadows become much nicer and softer. So depending on the type

33
00:02:56.020 --> 00:03:00.979
of scene that you're trying to get, you might want different values there. But in our case,

34
00:03:00.979 --> 00:03:06.419
as I say, source angle here, working to give us some nicer, softer shadows.

35
00:03:07.419 --> 00:03:11.069
That's the direction light. It's a pretty straightforward one. We go to the next one,

36
00:03:11.069 --> 00:03:15.729
which is a point light. When I drag the point light in, you can see it works the same way

37
00:03:15.729 --> 00:03:21.409
if you're familiar with any other DCC packages. It works the same as an omni light. So it's a

38
00:03:21.409 --> 00:03:27.069
light that from one point releases in all directions. So there we have it. Once again,

39
00:03:27.069 --> 00:03:32.960
we're going to want to set this to movable. And in this case, we can move and rotate this

40
00:03:35.090 --> 00:03:40.960
light. And the settings here are going to be the same. You can change the light color.

41
00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:45.199
The attenuation radius will determine how far that light travels from that point.

42
00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:52.030
And the intensity will determine the intensity of the light. You have, once again, some common

43
00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:56.750
options like the indirect light intensity. So again, how much it contributes to that bounce

44
00:03:56.750 --> 00:04:02.219
light in the scene. And again, this is all being handled automatically and dynamically

45
00:04:02.219 --> 00:04:06.620
by Lumen, which is really, really nice. No need to bake or anything like that.

46
00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:12.180
So next we have the spotlight, which would work exactly as you'd expect.

47
00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:16.019
Now, spotlight has a couple of interesting things that we can take a look at.

48
00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:20.060
First of all, we have an inner and outer cone angle. So the outer cone angle

49
00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:27.579
will determine how wide the cone of light that you're emitting from that spotlight is.

50
00:04:27.579 --> 00:04:31.500
So let's go ahead and put it against this wall here so we can see it a little bit better.

51
00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:39.730
So you can see as I change the cone angle to how that affects the shape of the light,

52
00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:46.139
I can just change the inner cone angle to change the fall off of the strength of that light.

53
00:04:46.139 --> 00:04:48.939
So I'm just going to press G to go into game mode here so we can see that

54
00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:53.810
happen in real time. You can see that adjust the fall off.

55
00:04:56.509 --> 00:05:05.379
And then we also have the source radius, which essentially determines a little bit in the same

56
00:05:05.379 --> 00:05:10.660
way that the source angle on the directional light determines the softness of the shadows.

57
00:05:11.379 --> 00:05:15.300
We can get the same sort of effect when it comes to spotlights and point lights

58
00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:18.740
changing the source radius. So this essentially changes the

59
00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:27.459
size of the light bulb, if you will, and will make for softer shadows, but also

60
00:05:27.459 --> 00:05:32.180
bigger specular reflections, essentially, because it has to take into account the size of the

61
00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:35.600
light bulb of the reflection. So that's another thing to keep in mind.

62
00:05:36.879 --> 00:05:41.600
Next, we have rect light or rectangular light, which is really, really useful light.

63
00:05:42.639 --> 00:05:46.079
And it has a couple of quirks of its own as well. So once again,

64
00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:52.620
changing the size of the source will make the shadow softer, but in this case,

65
00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:54.620
it also changes the shape of the light significantly.

66
00:05:54.620 --> 00:06:00.430
So let's go ahead and take a look at the rectangular light here.

67
00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:06.589
So I could change the length and the width, and you can really see how that affects

68
00:06:07.579 --> 00:06:11.089
the shape of the light in this case. And as the name suggests, you know,

69
00:06:11.089 --> 00:06:15.939
they're rectangular lights. And importantly, we also have the band or angle.

70
00:06:15.939 --> 00:06:26.300
So that's how steep the angle of the shape of the light that's actually getting shot out.

71
00:06:26.980 --> 00:06:32.019
And you can really help you sharpen up or essentially change the properties of the way

72
00:06:32.019 --> 00:06:36.180
in which the light's coming out. So the light is propagating out of this point here.

73
00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:39.540
And you can imagine this shape being essentially a piece of geometry that's

74
00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:45.899
affecting the propagation of that light. So that's the rectangular light.

75
00:06:45.899 --> 00:06:53.170
Once again, we also have typical values here, changing color, intensity,

76
00:06:54.480 --> 00:07:01.550
indirect lighting, as well as the attenuation. So how far that light travels from that spot.

77
00:07:04.769 --> 00:07:09.730
And last but not least, we have the skylight, which you probably use in most of your scenes,

78
00:07:10.689 --> 00:07:15.810
which allows for the calculation of that global contributions to the indirect lighting

79
00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:20.370
in the global illumination and sky shadowing as well. Again, I'm just going to set that to

80
00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:24.319
movable, like we've been doing with all the lights. And once again, in this case,

81
00:07:24.319 --> 00:07:28.800
you've got light color, indirect lighting intensity, and the intensity of the light itself.

82
00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:39.360
Let's go ahead and set it to 9.4. So the last thing I wanted to take a look at,

83
00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:44.160
because we've looked at most of the lights, is these two different light setups. Now,

84
00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:50.079
these two light setups are actually plugins that we've enabled. And they essentially come with a

85
00:07:50.079 --> 00:07:56.879
few extra bonuses. They're not just a straight up light type. So I'm going to drag the HDRI

86
00:07:56.879 --> 00:08:02.139
backdrop in here. And you can see it's a blueprint that includes an HDRI backdrop

87
00:08:02.139 --> 00:08:07.500
and comes with a skylight already. So you can put your model in here and take the screenshots.

88
00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:13.259
Now, the cool thing about this is that, as you can see already, the HDRI backdrop is actually

89
00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:20.060
contributing to the light and scene. Importantly, if you want to change that HDRI backdrop,

90
00:08:20.060 --> 00:08:24.379
you can change it here under the cube map section. You can also change its intensity,

91
00:08:25.019 --> 00:08:30.300
just like you'd imagine with all the other lights. However, the color will be determined

92
00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:36.220
by the cube map that you end up using. So no direct color changes. So that's the HDRI

93
00:08:36.220 --> 00:08:42.879
backdrop. The next thing is the sun and sky, which essentially is a blueprint that accurately

94
00:08:42.879 --> 00:08:47.200
mimics the sun and sky positions for a particular location and time on Earth.

95
00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:50.960
So if I have this, you'll notice that it blooms our screen out completely,

96
00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:58.000
and that's because it has a default value for the direction light that comes with it to 75,000

97
00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:03.120
lux. So in order for us to adjust to this, we need to change the exposure on our scene.

98
00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:08.720
So to do that, I'm going to go to the Lit tab here, or the View Mode tab. And instead of

99
00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:11.679
having the exposures at the game settings, I'm going to untick that,

100
00:09:12.450 --> 00:09:16.690
and I'm going to manually drag this up until it makes a little bit more sense for us.

101
00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:23.700
Now that I've done that, we can take a look at the blueprint itself, and you'll see

102
00:09:25.139 --> 00:09:28.740
it's got quite a lot of interesting things. You can choose a latitude and longitude

103
00:09:29.299 --> 00:09:34.100
for somewhere in the world, adjust the time zone for it, and then choose the date,

104
00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:39.460
and it will accurately mimic the lighting on that time of day, on that date,

105
00:09:39.460 --> 00:09:43.539
in that specific latitude and longitude. So really good if you're trying to make a more accurate

106
00:09:43.539 --> 00:09:48.100
simulation of what something might look like in a particular location. And again, this is actually

107
00:09:48.100 --> 00:09:51.700
something that's really handy for the architecture and construction industries, for example.

108
00:09:52.830 --> 00:09:56.429
Let's go ahead, and I'm going to undo those changes until we get our lights back,

109
00:09:58.049 --> 00:10:01.730
which we do. And I'm going to go back to our game settings. Let's go ahead and delete

110
00:10:01.730 --> 00:10:07.009
the HDRI backdrop. And those are the two, as I say, more advanced lighting setups that

111
00:10:07.009 --> 00:10:12.399
really are just blueprints that are set up to have particular properties.

112
00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:16.700
The last thing I want to take a look at, because we are looking at luminatheral,

113
00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:22.139
is the contribution to the lighting from emissive materials. So as you can see here,

114
00:10:22.139 --> 00:10:30.509
this light here has a material, and if I open it up, we'll open up the parent, you'll see it

115
00:10:30.509 --> 00:10:37.330
has emissive color assigned to it. So this value here is actually shooting past the value of

116
00:10:37.330 --> 00:10:42.960
one and emitting light itself. So if we wanted to create an object that was glowing and

117
00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:47.519
contributing to the lighting, let's say for example here, all we need to do is, for example,

118
00:10:47.519 --> 00:10:53.299
drop down, search for a material that has some glow on it, and assign it. And you can see,

119
00:10:53.299 --> 00:10:58.899
as soon as we do that, that material is already contributing to the global illumination of the

120
00:10:58.899 --> 00:11:07.580
scene. So that's it for our light types and a general simple introduction to the lighting

121
00:11:07.580 --> 00:11:12.480
tools inside Unreal Engine 5. I hope you enjoyed.
